{
 "hash": "1507b659d95f7d",
 "text": "The second our hands touched the ball, lightning shot straight between us. My fingers tingled. Kevin Durant shook his hand out. Next morning, I stepped on the court. I pulled up from mid-range. Swish. Again. Swish. I drove and dunked—I'd never even touched the net before. My middy was 100%. I destroyed tryouts, dropped 40 points on camera, and went viral overnight. Still, Kevin Durant's words stayed in my head. So I didn't get lazy. For months, I woke up at 5 AM—rain or snow—shooting 500 pull-up middies before school. Meanwhile, on TV, Kevin Durant was missing everything. Airballs. Bricks. Turnovers. The media said his career was over. I stared at my hands. I didn't just get his talent—I took it. The Thunder invited the \"viral miracle kid\" to suit up with KD. Before the fourth quarter, KD sat in the tunnel with his head down. I held out my signed ball. I'm sorry. This belongs to you. He grabbed it, and the lightning snapped right back. My arms felt heavy. The magic was gone. Five seconds left. Down by one. KD was instantly back—draining threes—so the other team sent three defenders straight at him. Coach pointed at me.",
 "voice": "leo",
 "lead": 0,
 "end": 76.718,
